<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Albulena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Ujëbardha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Albulena was fought on 2 September 1457 in central Albania, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> defeated a large Ottoman army under Isa Bey Evrenos. Skanderbeg had withdrawn into the mountains, letting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> spread out to plunder, before attacking their camp by surprise. The victory was among his greatest and brought him praise across Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-albulena.jpg" alt="Battle of Albulena" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dhimitër Frëngu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assault_on_Turkish_encampment.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> had fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> since 1444, and after more than a decade of war his resources were worn out. His siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> in 1455 ended in catastrophe when an Ottoman counterattack fell on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> during his temporary absence. Afterwards some of his most trusted officers deserted. Moisi Golemi returned in May 1456 at the head of an Ottoman force, was defeated at the Battle of Oranik and was taken back without punishment.</p><p>A graver loss was his nephew Hamza Kastrioti, one of his most important officers, who had grown dissatisfied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>'s increasing power and was promised control of much of Albania once it was conquered. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> were at their lowest point. Alfonso V of Aragon and Pope Calixtus III, his allies, could not provide for him fully, and a fleet the pope promised in 1457 did not arrive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>By the end of May 1457 an Ottoman army of between 50,000 and 80,000 men approached, against which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> had between 8,000 and 10,000. He stalled the akıncı vanguard marching through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a>, then withdrew before the main force. Since both Ottoman commanders knew his usual ambushes, he split his army into several groups and ordered them to march through the forests and mountains in different directions, supplied by the local people and by depots, and not to assemble or attack without his word.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> pillaged the Mat valley and camped at Albulena, north of Mount Tumenishta and south of modern Laç. Through July and August Skanderbeg stayed out of sight, and rumours spread that he had fled. On 2 September his reassembled army attacked the camp in three groups with a great noise of clashing metal. The Ottomans, believing the attackers more numerous than they were, panicked. Their dead may have been as many as 30,000, though it is unlikely that more than 15,000 were killed; 15,000 were taken prisoner and twenty-four standards were captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hamza Kastrioti was captured and sent as a prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. An Ottoman envoy came to ransom the standard bearers and forty distinguished prisoners and proposed a truce, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> would accept only if Svetigrad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> were restored to him. In 1460 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> and Skanderbeg signed an armistice that lasted three years, giving Skanderbeg the opportunity to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>. The historian Franz Babinger called the battle Skanderbeg's most brilliant victory. Desertions from his army became rare afterwards, but the war continued until the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a> in 1478.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Albulena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The league was a military alliance rather than a unified state: each lord kept his own lands, while Skanderbeg led a mobile army that used mountain terrain to defeat larger Ottoman forces, as at Torvioll in 1444 and <b>Albulena</b> in 1457.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a></li></ul></section>
